#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364332 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "42f47d95-9f20-5488-98c4-cb80278a7cf3" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Probability of a Geometric Random Variable on an Unbounded Interval"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "98d938b6-af68-5946-80a5-00573839d85f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.833" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364334}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "98d938b6-af68-5946-80a5-00573839d85f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.833" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364334} :answer-field/id #uuid "60e85f82-c79d-5023-b86a-469069558919" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364333}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "28c160b2-1422-5a1c-9b8b-0a7bcfaab4c6" :question/math-academy-id "q-332355" :question/problem "In a medical clinic, a certain rapid test detects a particular infection. On average, $9$ out of every $300$ patients who take the test receive a positive result. Each patient’s test result is independent of the others. Rounded to $3$ decimal places, the probability that the nurse will test more than $6$ patients before seeing the first positive result is{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "A geometric random variable $X$ with probability of success $p$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=(1-p)^{x-1}p\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the number of patients tested until the first positive result is obtained. Since the chance that any single patient tests positive is $3%,$ the number of patients tested until the first positive result can be modeled as a geometric random variable with a probability of success\n\n$$\np=\\frac{9}{300}=0.03.\n$$\n\nSo, $X∼\\text{Geom}(0.03).$ In this case, $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =(1-0.03)^{x-1}(0.03) \\\\  & =(0.97)^{x-1}(0.03)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo compute $P(X>6),$ we will use the complement:\n\n$$\nP(X>6)=1-P(X\\le 6)\n$$\n\nComputing the probability of the complement, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 6) & =P(X\\in {1,2,3,4,5,6}) \\\\  & =f(1)+f(2)+f(3)+f(4)+f(5)+f(6) \\\\  & =(0.97)^{1-1}(0.03)+(0.97)^{2-1}(0.03)+(0.97)^{3-1}(0.03) \\\\  & \\,+(0.97)^{4-1}(0.03)+(0.97)^{5-1}(0.03)+(0.97)^{6-1}(0.03) \\\\  & =[1+(0.97)+(0.97)^{2}+(0.97)^{3}+(0.97)^{4}+(0.97)^{5}](0.03) \\\\  & ≈0.167,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places.\n\nTherefore, the probability that the nurse will test more than $6$ patients before seeing the first positive result is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>6) & =1-P(X\\le 6) \\\\  & ≈1-0.167 \\\\  & ≈0.833,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places."}]}
